{
  "format": "wspradar.config",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "profile": {
    "id": "milazzo_tx_buddy",
    "title": {
      "en": "Dr. Milazzo Revisited: Extending an Early Multi-Part WSPR Antenna Study with Same-Cycle Evidence"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Technical article: Milazzo, C. F. / KP4MD (2011). Using the Weak Signal Propagation Reporter Network to Compare Antenna Performance](https://www.qsl.net/kp4md/wspr.htm)\n\nMilazzo’s work is one of the early detailed operator-led WSPR antenna studies, combining 40 m and 20 m transmit comparisons at VE6PDQ, reciprocal receive observations, and checks against VOACAP propagation predictions. This demo revisits one part — the 40 m KP4MD full-wave loop versus WB6RQN GAP Titan DX transmit comparison — and extends it from VE6PDQ to reporting receivers within 5,000 km over 18–20 December 2010, applying reported-power normalization, the Target-Active Gate, and same-cycle pairing.\n\nWithin this configured scope, the gated results contain **1,069 Only Target (KP4MD), 45 Joint, and 21 Only Reference (WB6RQN) peer-cycles**. The underlying non-joint reports — including VE6PDQ’s 62 Only Target records — remain available for inspection in Drill-Down. The Target-Active Gate retains a cycle only when KP4MD was reported by at least one receiver; it does not require both transmitters to be heard at every receiver.\n\nBecause KP4MD operated continuously on the frequency under study while WB6RQN frequency-hopped, the non-joint reports cannot isolate antenna performance. The narrower synchronized subset contains **45 joint reports from nine shared cycles**, with median ΔSNR (KP4MD loop − WB6RQN GAP Titan DX), normalized for reported transmit power, of **−4 dB**. At VE6PDQ itself, only one same-cycle pair is available, giving **−2 dB** after power normalization.\n\nThis archive reconstruction illustrates why operating schedules, gating, non-joint evidence, and synchronized comparisons must be interpreted separately. A separate point-by-point check reconciles all **44 Figure 6 markers** with the **reciprocal RX direction: VE6PDQ transmitting to KP4MD and WB6RQN**. All annotated SNR values agree exactly, with times within two minutes of the graphical readout, despite the figure title and caption describing reception at VE6PDQ. This direction-labelling discrepancy means Figure 6 provides an RX reference, not numerical validation of this TX demo. The result therefore extends the original study without claiming to reproduce its numerical transmit antenna-performance conclusions."
    }
  },
